C("mahabhisha","Mahabhisha","","muh-haa-BHI-shaa","male","human (king, former birth of Santanu)","king in heaven",
  "Ikshvaku king who stares at Ganga in Brahma's court and is reborn as Santanu",
  "A king of the Ikshvaku race who reached heaven by sacrifices. When Ganga's garment slips in Brahma's court he looks at her, and Brahma curses him to be reborn on earth; he chooses to be born as Pratipa's son (§96).",
  "bold|smitten","fascination",f"An Indian king, warm baritone, {A}","96 97","non_speaking")
C("pratipa","Pratipa","","pruh-TEE-puh","male","human (Kuru king)","elderly king",
  "Kuru king; father of Santanu; refuses Ganga for himself and reserves her for his son",
  "Ganga sits on his right thigh and asks him to marry her. He refuses, because the right thigh is for daughters and daughters-in-law, and accepts her as his future daughter-in-law (§97). He performs austerities for a son, Santanu, whom he tells to accept the celestial woman unquestioningly, and he retires to the forest (§97).",
  "virtuous|restrained|fatherly","dignified gentleness|fatherly counsel",
  f"An elderly Indian king, dignified gentle baritone, {A}","96 97")
C("santanu","Santanu","","SHUN-tuh-noo","male","human (Kuru king)","young→elderly king",
  "Kuru king; husband of Ganga and of Satyavati; father of Bhishma, Chitrangada and Vichitravirya",
  "Mahabhisha reborn as Pratipa's son (§96–§97). He marries Ganga, who must never be questioned; she drowns seven sons until he protests at the eighth and she leaves (§98–§99). She returns Devavrata grown and trained (§100). He pines for the fisherman's daughter Satyavati, and Devavrata wins her for him with his vow, for which Santanu grants him death at will (§100). By Satyavati he fathers Chitrangada and Vichitravirya, and then he dies (§101).",
  "noble|passionate|melancholic|just","love|grief|longing|gratitude",
  f"A mature Indian king, rich regal baritone, warm but touched with melancholy, {A}","97 98 99 100 101")
C("bhishma","Bhishma","Devavrata|Gangeya|son of Ganga|son of Santanu","BHEESH-muh","male","human (Kuru prince; Vasu incarnate)","young man→elder patriarch",
  "Son of Santanu and Ganga; takes the terrible vow; regent and patriarch of the Kurus",
  "The eighth Vasu, born to Ganga and trained by her (§99–§100). To win Satyavati for his father he renounces the throne and vows lifelong celibacy; the gods shower flowers and call him Bhishma, and Santanu grants him death at will (§100). As regent he defeats the kings at the Kasi swayamvara and carries off Amba, Ambika and Ambalika, defeating Salya/Salwa; he lets Amba go (§102). He refuses Satyavati's plea to beget heirs himself, and suggests a Brahmana (§103–§105). He raises Dhritarashtra, Pandu and Vidura (§109), arranges their marriages to Gandhari, Madri and others (§110, §113–§114), and appoints Drona as the princes' preceptor (§131, §133). He tells of Dirghatamas (§104). He counsels the restoration of the Pandavas and a half-kingdom (§206).",
  "dutiful|resolute|self-sacrificing|wise","solemn resolve|stern authority|tender concern|grief",
  f"A powerful Indian warrior prince in his prime who ages into the patriarch, commanding low voice, noble and resolute, {A}","100 102 103 104 105 109 110 113 133 206")
C("satyavati","Satyavati","Gandhavati|Yojanagandha|daughter of the fisherman|Kali","SUT-yuh-vuh-tee","female","human (fisher-raised queen)","maiden→queen mother",
  "Fisherman's adopted daughter; mother of Vyasa by Parasara; queen of Santanu; mother of Chitrangada and Vichitravirya",
  "Born of a fish from Uparichara Vasu's seed and raised by the fisherman chief (§63). As a ferry maiden she bears Vyasa to Parasara, regains her virginity and loses her fishy smell (§63, §105). Santanu sees her rowing (§100), and Bhishma's vow wins her for the king (§100). After her sons' deaths she asks Bhishma to beget heirs and then recalls Vyasa (§103, §105). She sends Ambika and Ambalika to him (§105–§106). After Pandu's death Vyasa advises her to retire to the forest, and she does so with Ambika and Ambalika (§128).",
  "shrewd|resilient|ambitious for her line|maternal","composure|grief|persuasion|anxiety",
  f"A fisherman's daughter who becomes queen-mother, bright clear voice maturing into a shrewd measured alto, {A}","63 100 103 105 106 128")
C("dasa","Chief of the fishermen","the fisherman|Dasa","DAA-shuh","male","human (fisher chieftain)","older man",
  "Foster-father of Satyavati who demands that her son inherit the throne",
  "Santanu asks for Satyavati, and the chief of the fishermen demands that her son be heir (§100). When Devavrata comes he presses further about Devavrata's future sons, provoking the vow of celibacy; the hair on his body stands on end with joy as he bestows his daughter (§100).",
  "shrewd|bargaining|protective","calculating|delighted",
  f"An older Indian fisherman chieftain, earthy slightly gravelly voice, shrewd and deliberate, {A}","100","single_section_speaker")
C("chitrangada_kuru","Chitrangada (son of Santanu)","Chitrangada","chi-TRAANG-guh-duh","male","human (Kuru king)","young king",
  "Elder son of Santanu and Satyavati; killed by a Gandharva of the same name",
  "Born of Satyavati to Santanu. As king he defeats all, and is killed by a mighty Gandharva of the same name after a three-year battle on the field of Kurukshetra (§101–§102).",
  "proud|valiant","pride",f"A proud young Indian king, ringing baritone, {A}","101 102","non_speaking")
C("vichitravirya","Vichitravirya","","vi-CHI-truh-VEER-yuh","male","human (Kuru king)","young king",
  "Younger son of Santanu and Satyavati; husband of Ambika and Ambalika; dies childless",
  "Becomes king as a minor under Bhishma's regency (§102). Married to Ambika and Ambalika, he is devoted to pleasure and dies of consumption, childless (§102).",
  "pleasant|frail|indulgent","youthful ease",f"A youthful Indian king, light pleasant tenor, a little frail, {A}","101 102 105","non_speaking")
C("ambika","Ambika","Amvika","UM-bi-kaa","female","human (Kasi princess)","young widow",
  "Kasi princess, wife of Vichitravirya; by Vyasa mother of the blind Dhritarashtra",
  "Carried off by Bhishma and married to Vichitravirya (§102). Sent to Vyasa by Satyavati, she shuts her eyes in fear at his grim form, and so her son Dhritarashtra is born blind (§106). Sent again, she substitutes a maidservant, who bears Vidura (§106). She retires to the forest with Satyavati (§128).",
  "timid|fearful","fear|shrinking",f"A young Indian princess, soft timid voice, {A}","102 106 128","non_speaking")
C("ambalika","Ambalika","Kausalya","um-BAA-li-kaa","female","human (Kasi princess)","young widow",
  "Kasi princess, wife of Vichitravirya; by Vyasa mother of the pale Pandu",
  "Married to Vichitravirya (§102). She turns pale at the sight of Vyasa, and her son Pandu is born pale (§106). She rejoices at Pandu's conquests (§114). Satyavati takes her (as Kausalya) to the forest after Pandu's death (§128).",
  "delicate|fearful","pallid fear|joy",f"A young Indian princess, delicate breathy voice, {A}","102 106 114 128","non_speaking")
C("dirghatamas","Dirghatamas","son of Utathya","deer-guh-TUH-mus","male","sage","blind Rishi",
  "Blind son of Utathya; father of Kakshivat and others by Vali's queen's Sudra maid",
  "Cursed in the womb by Vrihaspati to be born blind (§104). He sets the rule that a woman should keep to one husband, is set adrift by his sons, and is rescued by king Vali. Vali's queen Sudeshna sends her Sudra nurse instead, and Dirghatamas tells Vali that the eleven sons with Kakshivat as eldest are his (§104). Bhishma tells this story as precedent (§104).",
  "fierce|proud|blunt","indignation|bluntness",f"A blind old Indian sage, harsh blunt voice, {A}","104","single_section_speaker")
C("mandavya","Mandavya","Animandavya","MAAN-duh-vyuh","male","sage","old ascetic",
  "Rishi impaled though innocent; curses Dharma to be born as Vidura",
  "Under a vow of silence he is impaled after thieves hide stolen goods in his hermitage, and survives on the stake (§107). Told he was punished for piercing insects with grass as a child, he curses Dharma to be born of a Sudra woman, and sets the age of responsibility at fourteen (§108).",
  "stoic|just|wrathful when wronged","silent endurance|righteous wrath",f"A gaunt old Indian ascetic, low stoic voice, {A}","106 107 108","single_section_speaker")
C("dhritarashtra","Dhritarashtra","son of Ambika|son of Vichitravirya","dhri-tuh-RAASH-truh","male","human (Kuru king)","young prince→aged blind king (old by his own account in the §1 lament: 'because I am old')",
  "Blind elder son of Vyasa and Ambika; king of the Kurus; father of Duryodhana and ninety-nine others",
  "Born blind because Ambika closed her eyes (§106). Passed over for the throne because of his blindness, he marries Gandhari (§109–§110). He fathers a hundred sons, Duhsala, and Yuyutsu by a Vaisya maid (§115–§116). He becomes ruler after Pandu (§126–§127). He grows jealous of the Pandavas, takes Kanika's crooked counsel (§142), and at Duryodhana's urging sends them to Varanavata (§143–§145). He outwardly mourns them (§152). On hearing of the swayamvara he first rejoices, thinking Duryodhana has won (§202). He later agrees to give the Pandavas half the kingdom at Khandavaprastha (§209).",
  "conflicted|weak-willed|loving father|envious","anxiety|longing|veiled envy|false cheer|regret|grief|despair|self-justification",
  f"An aged blind Indian king, deep resonant voice worn by age and sorrow with a faint tremor, bitter and self-justifying, breaking into grief, {A}","106 110 115 142 143 144 152 202 209")
C("pandu","Pandu","","PAAN-doo","male","human (Kuru king)","young king",
  "Pale son of Vyasa and Ambalika; conquering king cursed by Kindama; father (by gods) of the Pandavas",
  "Born pale because Ambalika paled (§106). He marries Kunti at her swayamvara (§112), and Bhishma buys Madri for him (§113). He conquers many kingdoms (§113–§114) and retires to the forest (§114). He shoots the Rishi Kindama mating in deer form and is cursed to die if he touches a woman in passion (§118). He renounces the world (§119), sets out with the Rishis (§120), and asks Kunti to bear sons by the gods (§120–§123). He dies in Madri's embrace (§125).",
  "noble|melancholic|pious|passionate","valour|remorse|longing|resignation",
  f"An Indian warrior king, clear noble baritone touched with melancholy, {A}","106 112 113 118 119 120 123 125")
C("vidura","Vidura","Kshatta|Kshattri","VI-doo-ruh","male","human (Kuru counsellor; Dharma incarnate)","young→mature counsellor",
  "Son of Vyasa by a Sudra maidservant; Dharma incarnate; wise counsellor and protector of the Pandavas",
  "Dharma born under Mandavya's curse (§106, §108). He tells Dhritarashtra to abandon the newborn Duryodhana (§115). He warns Yudhishthira in a riddling tongue about the house of lac (§147), sends a miner to dig the tunnel (§149), and sends a boat across the Ganga (§151). He tells Dhritarashtra of the Pandavas' marriage (§202) and urges restoring them (§208). He brings them back to Hastinapura (§209).",
  "wise|righteous|discreet|loyal","measured counsel|grave warning|quiet joy",
  f"A wise Indian counsellor, measured clear baritone, discreet and calm, {A}","106 108 115 147 149 151 202 208 209")
C("gandhari","Gandhari","daughter of Suvala","gaan-DHAA-ree","female","human (Gandhara princess)","young bride→queen",
  "Daughter of Suvala; blindfolds herself for her blind husband Dhritarashtra; mother of the hundred Kauravas and Duhsala",
  "Granted a hundred sons by Siva (§110). She blindfolds her eyes for life on marrying the blind king (§110). Jealous at Kunti's firstborn, she strikes her womb and delivers a ball of flesh, which divides into a hundred and one parts, kept in pots (§115–§116).",
  "devoted|austere|proud|impatient","controlled dignity|jealous anguish",
  f"A mature Indian queen, low controlled alto, austere and dignified, {A}","110 115 116","non_speaking")
C("sakuni","Sakuni","son of Suvala","SHUH-koo-nee","male","human (Gandhara prince)","adult",
  "Brother of Gandhari; Duryodhana's evil counsellor",
  "Brings Gandhari to the Kurus and gives her to Dhritarashtra (§110). He conspires with Duryodhana, Karna and Duhsasana against the Pandavas (§129, §143), joins the poisoning plots (§129), and offers counsel against Drupada (§202).",
  "cunning|scheming","sly insinuation|calculation",f"A cunning Indian prince, oily insinuating tenor, {A}","110 129 143","non_speaking")
C("kunti","Kunti","Pritha|daughter of Kuntibhoja|daughter of Sura","KOON-tee","female","human (Yadava princess, Kuru queen)","maiden→mother of grown sons",
  "Daughter of Sura adopted by Kuntibhoja; mother of Karna by Surya and of Yudhishthira, Bhima and Arjuna by the gods",
  "Given as a child to Kuntibhoja (§111). She pleases Durvasa and receives a mantra, tries it, and bears Karna to Surya; she casts him into the river (§111). She chooses Pandu at her swayamvara (§112). At his request she invokes Dharma, Vayu and Indra (§122–§123) and shares the mantra with Madri (§124). She raises Madri's sons after Pandu's death (§125–§126). She escapes the house of lac (§148–§150), and at Ekachakra offers Bhima to save the Brahmana's family (§162–§163). Her unknowing 'Enjoy ye all (what ye have obtained)' leads to Draupadi becoming the wife of all five (§193, §197). She swoons at the tournament on recognising Karna, and Vidura revives her (§138).",
  "dignified|resilient|protective|secretive","maternal anguish|composure|tender resolve|guilt",
  f"A young Indian queen who matures into a resilient mother, gentle yet dignified voice, {A}","111 112 122 123 125 138 148 162 163 193 197")
C("kuntibhoja","Kuntibhoja","","KOON-tee-bho-juh","male","human (king)","elder",
  "Childless king who adopts Pritha (Kunti)",
  "Sura's cousin and friend, who adopts Pritha (§111). He holds her swayamvara and gives her to Pandu with gifts (§112).",
  "kindly|generous","warmth",f"A kindly elderly Indian king, mellow voice, {A}","111 112","non_speaking")
C("durvasa","Durvasa","","door-VAA-suh","male","sage","ancient",
  "Irascible Rishi who gives Kunti the god-invoking mantra",
  "Pleased by Pritha's service he gives her a mantra to summon any god (§111, §123). Sent by Rudra, he officiates at king Swetaki's twelve-year sacrifice (§225).",
  "irascible|testing|generous to devotees","prickly|benevolent",f"A crusty old Indian sage, sharp crackling voice, {A}","111 123 225","non_speaking")
C("madri","Madri","princess of Madra|sister of Salya","MAA-dree","female","human (Madra princess, Kuru queen)","young queen",
  "Sister of Salya; second wife of Pandu; mother of Nakula and Sahadeva by the Aswins",
  "Bought for Pandu from Salya by Bhishma (§113). She asks Pandu to have Kunti share the mantra, and bears the twins by the Aswins (§124). Pandu dies embracing her; she ascends his funeral pyre, entrusting her sons to Kunti (§125).",
  "affectionate|loyal|guilt-stricken","warmth|desire|grief|resolve",
  f"A young Indian queen, warm bright soprano, affectionate, breaking with grief, {A}","113 124 125")
C("salya","Salya","king of Madra|brother of Madri","SHUL-yuh","male","human (king)","adult king",
  "King of Madra; brother of Madri; suitor at Draupadi's swayamvara",
  "Gives his sister Madri to Bhishma for wealth, in keeping with his family custom (§113). At Draupadi's swayamvara he fails to string the bow (§190) and fights Bhima, who throws him (§192). (KMG §102 also calls the Saubha king who fights Bhishma 'Salya'; that is Salwa, a different person.)",
  "proud|bound by custom","proud bargaining|battle fury",f"A proud Indian king, robust baritone, {A}","113 190 192","non_speaking")
C("satasringa_rishis","Rishis of Satasringa","rishis|ascetics|Rishis of the hundred peaks","RISH-eez (shuh-tuh-SHRING-uh)","male","chorus (sages of the mountain of a hundred peaks)","ascetics of the Himalaya",
  "Rishis of the mountain of a hundred peaks (Sata-sringa) among whom Pandu lives as an ascetic",
  "Pandu reaches Sata-sringa and lives among its ascetics, who call him brother, friend and son (§120). When they set out for Brahman's abode they dissuade him from following with his wives and foretell godlike progeny (§120). They perform the birth rites of the five Pandavas and name them (§124). After Pandu's death they resolve to take his children to Hastinapura, and their eldest presents them to the Kurus before the Rishis vanish (§126).",
  "kindly|wise|protective","benevolent|grave|solemn",f"Shares the Naimisha Rishis group voice (decision 2026-10-10): gentle chorus of elderly Indian ascetics, {A}","120 124 126","chorus",voice_group="naimisha_rishis")
C("kindama","Kindama","the deer|Rishi Kindama","KIN-duh-muh","male","sage (in deer form)","Rishi",
  "Rishi who, mating in the form of a deer, is shot by Pandu and curses him",
  "Pandu shoots a deer mating with its mate; dying, the deer reveals himself as the Rishi Kindama and curses Pandu to die when he unites with a woman (§118).",
  "reproachful|dignified in death","pain|bitter reproach",f"A dying Indian sage, pained reproachful voice, {A}","118","single_section_speaker")
C("karna","Karna","Vasusena|Radheya|son of Radha|Kama (misprint in §138)","KUR-nuh","male","human (Kunti's son, raised by a Suta)","youth",
  "Son of Kunti and Surya, raised by the Suta Adhiratha and Radha; Duryodhana's friend and Arjuna's rival",
  "Born with natural armour and ear-rings, cast into the river by Pritha, and raised by Radha and her husband, who name him Vasusena (§111). He becomes Drona's pupil (§134). He challenges Arjuna at the tournament; when Kripa demands his lineage, Duryodhana makes him king of Anga and Karna pledges friendship (§138). His aged father Adhiratha enters trembling, Karna bows to him, and Bhima jeers (§139). He conspires against the Pandavas (§143). At the swayamvara Draupadi refuses him, and he later fights Arjuna (§189, §192). He urges war against Drupada (§203–§204).",
  "proud|generous|wounded|loyal","defiant pride|wounded dignity|fierce loyalty",
  f"A proud young Indian warrior, strong resonant baritone with a wounded edge, {A}","111 134 138 139 189 192 203")
C("adhiratha","Adhiratha","husband of Radha|the Suta","UDH-i-ruh-tuh","male","human (Suta charioteer)","old man",
  "Suta charioteer, Karna's foster-father",
  "Takes up the infant cast into the water and, with his wife Radha, brings him up as their own (§111). He enters the tournament trembling and leaning on a staff, and Karna bows to him; Bhima jeers at his low birth (§139).",
  "humble|loving","trembling joy",f"An old Indian charioteer, humble shaky voice, {A}","111 139","non_speaking")
C("radha","Radha","","RAA-dhaa","female","human (Suta woman)","adult",
  "Karna's foster-mother",
  "With her husband she adopts the river-borne infant and names him Vasusena (§111). Karna is known as 'Radha's son' (§134, §190).",
  "loving","maternal warmth",f"A warm Indian mother, homely voice, {A}","111 134","non_speaking")
C("yudhishthira","Yudhishthira","Dharmaraja|son of Dharma|Ajatasatru","yoo-DHISH-thi-ruh","male","human (Pandava prince)","boy→young king",
  "Eldest Pandava, son of Dharma; embodiment of righteousness",
  "Begotten by Dharma on Kunti (§123). Dhritarashtra installs him as heir-apparent (§141), and Vidura warns him in a riddling tongue about the house of lac (§147–§148). He agrees to Kunti's plan at Ekachakra after first objecting (§164). He spares the Gandharva at Kumbhinasi's plea (§172). He insists that all five marry Draupadi (§197) and argues dharma with Drupada and Vyasa (§197–§198). He is crowned at Khandavaprastha (§209), and hears Narada (§210).",
  "righteous|calm|forbearing|deliberate","steady calm|grave concern|gentle reproach",
  f"A young Indian prince, steady gentle baritone, righteous and composed, {A}","123 141 147 148 164 197 209 210")
C("bhima","Bhima","Bhimasena|Vrikodara","BHEE-muh","male","human (Pandava prince; son of Vayu)","boy→young man",
  "Second Pandava, son of Vayu; of immense strength and appetite; kills Hidimva and Vaka",
  "Born of Vayu (§123), he falls from Kunti's lap and shatters the rock (§123). He bullies the Kaurava boys (§128). Poisoned and thrown into the Ganga by Duryodhana, he is healed and strengthened by the Nagas (§128). He carries his family from the burning house of lac (§148–§150, §153). He kills Hidimva and marries Hidimva's sister, fathering Ghatotkacha (§154–§157). He kills Vaka at Ekachakra (§163–§166), and fights Salya at the swayamvara (§192).",
  "boisterous|impulsive|protective|fierce","booming mirth|towering rage|tenderness to family",
  f"A huge young Indian warrior, booming hearty bass, impulsive and warm, {A}","123 128 150 153 155 156 157 165 166 192")
C("arjuna","Arjuna","Dhananjaya|Phalguna|Partha|Kiriti|Vibhatsu|Jishnu|Savyasachin","UR-joo-nuh","male","human (Pandava prince; son of Indra)","boy→young hero",
  "Third Pandava, son of Indra; Drona's foremost pupil; wins Draupadi; burns Khandava with Krishna",
  "Born of Indra, announced by a heavenly voice (§123). Drona's favourite, he shoots the bird's eye (§134) and saves Drona from a crocodile, receiving the Brahmasira (§135). He shines at the tournament (§137), captures Drupada (§140), and defeats the Gandharva Angaraparna (§172). He wins Draupadi by piercing the target (§190–§191). He goes into exile for breaking the Draupadi rule (§215), marries Ulupi and Chitrangada (§216–§217), frees the cursed Apsaras including Varga (§218–§219), and carries off Subhadra (§221–§223). With Krishna he burns the Khandava forest, defeating Indra (§224–§230).",
  "disciplined|chivalrous|brilliant|loyal","confidence|ardour|filial reverence|battle exultation",
  f"A heroic young Indian archer-prince, clear confident baritone, warm and disciplined, {A}","123 134 135 137 140 172 190 215 216 217 221 229")
C("nakula","Nakula","","NUH-koo-luh","male","human (Pandava prince; son of the Aswins)","boy→young man",
  "Fourth Pandava, twin son of Madri by the Aswins",
  "Born of Madri by the Aswins (§124). Raised by Kunti after Madri's death (§126). Shares the Pandavas' trials: the house of lac (§150) and marriage to Draupadi (§197).",
  "handsome|gentle|loyal","quiet warmth",f"A handsome young Indian prince, smooth light baritone, {A}","124 126 150 197","non_speaking")
C("sahadeva","Sahadeva","","suh-huh-DAY-vuh","male","human (Pandava prince; son of the Aswins)","boy→young man",
  "Fifth Pandava, twin son of Madri by the Aswins",
  "Born of Madri by the Aswins (§124). Raised by Kunti after Madri's death (§126). Shares the Pandavas' trials: the house of lac (§150) and marriage to Draupadi (§197).",
  "reserved|wise|loyal","quiet thoughtfulness",f"A reserved young Indian prince, soft thoughtful tenor, {A}","124 126 150 197","non_speaking")
C("duryodhana","Duryodhana","Suyodhana","door-YO-dhuh-nuh","male","human (Kaurava prince)","boy→young prince",
  "Eldest son of Dhritarashtra and Gandhari; implacable enemy of the Pandavas",
  "Born with evil omens; Vidura urges abandoning him (§115). Jealous of Bhima, he poisons him and throws him into the Ganga (§128–§129). He makes Karna king of Anga (§138), urges his father to banish the Pandavas (§143–§144), and has Purochana build the house of lac (§146). Defeated in the attempt on Drupada (§140) and humiliated at the swayamvara (§192), he plots again (§202–§203).",
  "envious|proud|scheming|fiercely loyal to friends","seething envy|arrogance|scheming calm|loyalty",
  f"A proud young Indian prince, sharp forceful baritone edged with envy, {A}","115 128 138 143 144 146 202 203")
